{#
	ACF layout: timeline — Figma `timeline` (15:95) on `about`.

	An ordered list, because the order is the content. Each row is the year on
	the start edge and the entry beside it; in the DOM the year comes first,
	which under dir="rtl" puts it on the right — where the frame draws it — and
	makes a screen reader say the year before the milestone.

	The year is authored, not generated: it is a fact about the entry, not its
	index. What is generated is the heading level: the `section-header` carries
	the h2 and the entry titles are h3. A row saved before the header existed has
	no heading, and then the titles step up to h2 rather than leaving an h3 with
	no h2 above it.

	No motion of its own. Timelines usually animate as they scroll; the
	behaviour map is closed and has no row for it, so this gets the ordinary
	section reveal and nothing else.
#}

{% if section.entries|length %}
	{% set title_tag = section.heading ? 'h3' : 'h2' %}

	<section class="timeline">
		<div class="container timeline__inner">

			{% if section.heading %}
				<div class="timeline__header">
					{% include 'partials/ui/section-header.twig' with {
						eyebrow: section.eyebrow,
						heading: section.heading,
						intro: section.intro
					} only %}
				</div>
			{% endif %}

			<ol class="timeline__list" role="list">
				{% for entry in section.entries %}
					{% if entry.title %}
						<li class="timeline__row">
							{% if entry.year %}
								<p class="timeline__year">{{ entry.year }}</p>
							{% endif %}

							<div class="timeline__entry">
								<{{ title_tag }} class="timeline__title">{{ entry.title }}</{{ title_tag }}>

								{% if entry.text %}
									<p class="timeline__text">{{ entry.text }}</p>
								{% endif %}
							</div>
						</li>
					{% endif %}
				{% endfor %}
			</ol>

		</div>
	</section>
{% endif %}
